</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-substitution-examples">
<title>Choosing a Transformation</title>

<p>
In practice we usually run the process in the other direction: the
region of integration <m>R</m> is awkward in <m>xy</m>-coordinates, and
we look for a substitution under which it corresponds to a simple
region<mdash/>ideally a rectangle<mdash/>with constant limits of
integration. The boundary curves of <m>R</m> usually tell us what
<m>u</m> and <m>v</m> should be.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-parallelogram-substitution">
<title>A Parallelogram Region</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the transformation <m>T(u,v) = (u - 2v,\, v)</m> to compute
</p>

<me>
\iint_D (x + 3y)\, dx\, dy,
</me>

<p>
where <m>D</m> is the parallelogram bounded by the lines
<m>x + 2y = 6</m>, <m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m>,
shown in <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Read <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> off <m>T</m> and compute the Jacobian by
<xref ref="eq-jacobian-2d"/>. Invert the transformation to get
<m>u</m> and <m>v</m> in terms of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and translate
each of the four boundary lines; the parallelogram should become a
rectangle, shown in <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub"/>. Rewrite the
integrand in <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>, apply <xref ref="eq-sub-2d"/>, and
evaluate. Compare with <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11.5) -- (15,11.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$x =$ \quad $y =$ \qquad $\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.2) {inverse: $u =$ \quad $v =$ \qquad the four lines become: \qquad so $R =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {integrand: $x + 3y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$\displaystyle\iint_D (x+3y)\,dx\,dy = \iint_R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the transformation and its Jacobian, for the inverse and the rectangle, for the integrand in the new variables, and for the substituted integral and its evaluation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub-video">
<caption>
The substitution straightens the parallelogram <m>D</m> into a
rectangle. Watch what happens to areas, and compare with the Jacobian
you computed.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-parallelogram-sub"
       source="videos/parallelogram_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub">
<caption>
The parallelogram <m>D</m> between the lines <m>x + 2y = 6</m>,
<m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m> (left), and the
corresponding rectangle <m>R</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 44%" valign="middle">
<image>
<shortdescription>
A parallelogram between two slanted lines and two horizontal lines.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parallelogram-D">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="10">
<definition>l1(x) = (6 - x)/2</definition>
<definition>l2(x) = (10 - x)/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-0.8,11.5,5.8)">
<grid-axes at="pd-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="pd-region" points="((4,1),(8,1),(4,3),(0,3))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="none"/>
<graph at="pd-line1" function="l1" domain="(-0.8,11)" stroke="#d02090"
       thickness="2"/>
<graph at="pd-line2" function="l2" domain="(-0.8,11)" stroke="#d02090"
       thickness="2"/>
