       color="#d02090">
<m>x+2y=10</m>
</label>
<label at="pd-l1-label" anchor="(1.5,0.9)" alignment="se"
       color="#d02090">
<m>x+2y=6</m>
</label>
<label at="pd-D" anchor="(4,2)" alignment="c">
<m>D</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two parallel lines with slope negative one half, x plus 2 y equals 6 and x plus 2 y equals 10, drawn in pink. Between them, the horizontal lines y equals 1 and y equals 3 cut off a parallelogram D, shaded tan.">
  <annotation ref="pd-region" text="The parallelogram D, bounded by the two pink lines and the horizontal lines y equals 1 and y equals 3."/>
  <annotation ref="pd-line1" text="The line x plus 2 y equals 6, which becomes u equals 6 after the substitution."/>
  <annotation ref="pd-line2" text="The line x plus 2 y equals 10, which becomes u equals 10 after the substitution."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
The rectangle R in the u v plane, image of the parallelogram.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parallelogram-R">
<diagram dimensions="(280,240)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(4.4,-0.8,11.5,5.8)">
<grid-axes at="pr-grid" xlabel="u" ylabel="v"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="pr-rect" points="((6,1),(10,1),(10,3),(6,3))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="#c07020" thickness="2"/>
<label at="pr-R" anchor="(8,2)" alignment="c">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The rectangle R in the u v plane, from u equals 6 to u equals 10 and v equals 1 to v equals 3, shaded tan. It is the image of the parallelogram D under the substitution.">
  <annotation ref="pr-rect" text="The rectangle R, with constant limits 6 to 10 in u and 1 to 3 in v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-curved-to-rectangle">
<title>A Curved Region Mapped to a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>D</m> be the region in the first quadrant bounded by the
hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = -3</m>, <m>x^2 - y^2 = 3</m>, <m>xy = 1</m>,
and <m>xy = 4</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-curved-region"/>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a transformation <m>T</m> that relates <m>D</m> to a rectangle
<m>R</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the integral <m>\displaystyle \iint_D xy\, (x^2 + y^2)\, dx\, dy</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(In <alert>A</alert>, let the boundary curves suggest <m>u</m> and
<m>v</m>: each pair of curves should become a pair of lines
<m>u = \text{const}</m> or <m>v = \text{const}</m>. Describe the
rectangle <m>R</m>; compare with <xref ref="fig-curved-region"/>. In
<alert>B</alert>, the substitution is given in the direction
<m>u = u(x,y)</m>, <m>v = v(x,y)</m>, so first compute
<m>\partial(u,v)/\partial(x,y)</m>; by the inverse function theorem
the Jacobian you need, <m>\partial(x,y)/\partial(u,v)</m>, is its
reciprocal. Then apply <xref ref="eq-sub-2d"/>: a factor in the
integrand cancels against the Jacobian, and what is left is a function
of <m>v</m> alone. Compare with <xref ref="fig-curved-region-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {A. $u =$ \quad $v =$ \qquad $R =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {B. $\dfrac{\partial(u,v)}{\partial(x,y)} =$ \qquad so \qquad $\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$\displaystyle\iint_D xy\,(x^2+y^2)\,dx\,dy = \iint_R$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {evaluate:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the substitution and the rectangle, for the Jacobian in the easy direction and its reciprocal, for the substituted integral, and for its evaluation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region-video">
<caption>
The substitution maps the region between the four hyperbolas onto a
rectangle. Watch what the Jacobian does to the integrand.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-curved-region-sub"
       source="videos/curved_region_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region">
<caption>
The region <m>D</m> bounded by the hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = \pm 3</m>
and <m>xy = 1</m>, <m>xy = 4</m> (left) corresponds to a rectangle
<m>R</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
